    "export.readme": "# iOS TestFlight deployment settings backup\n\nGenerated: {date}\nBundle ID: {bundleId}\nTeam ID: {teamId}\n\n## 📁 File layout\n\n```\n├── certificate.p12              # distribution certificate (Base64-decoded)\n├── provisioning.mobileprovision # provisioning profile (Base64-decoded)\n├── api-key.p8                   # App Store Connect API Key (Base64-decoded)\n├── github-secrets/              # values for GitHub Secrets\n│   ├── APPLE_CERTIFICATE_BASE64.txt\n│   ├── APPLE_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD.txt\n│   ├── APPLE_PROVISIONING_PROFILE_BASE64.txt\n│   ├── IOS_PROVISIONING_PROFILE_NAME.txt\n│   ├── APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY_BASE64.txt\n│   ├── APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY_ID.txt\n│   ├── APP_STORE_CONNECT_ISSUER_ID.txt\n│   ├── APPLE_TEAM_ID.txt\n│   └── IOS_BUNDLE_ID.txt\n└── README.md\n```\n\n## 🔐 Registering GitHub Secrets\n\n1. GitHub repository → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions\n2. Register the contents of each file in the `github-secrets/` folder as a secret\n3. Use the file name without .txt as the secret name\n4. Once the secrets are registered, delete the downloaded github-secrets files and folder. They contain your certificate and keys as is\n\n## ⚠️ Cautions\n\n- These files contain sensitive information\n- Delete the github-secrets files once the secrets are registered. They contain your certificate and keys as is\n- Keep them somewhere safe and never commit them to Git\n- Encrypt them if needed\n",
